  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe current surface pattern features a sharp high pressure system\ncentered over northern Quebec and extending down the length of \nLake Michigan, keeping dry air with precipitable water values 40 \nto 60 percent of normal over eastern Wisconsin. Meanwhile, a \ndeeper moisture plume and scattered light showers over western \nWisconsin are slowly shifting eastward against the Great Lakes \nridge axis. While these showers will likely dissipate this \nafternoon as they run into the very dry airmass, cloud cover will \nlikely keep temperatures cooler than yesterday. Focus of this \nforecast remains on cloud cover and dense fog potential tonight. \n\nPatchy Dense Fog Tonight And Monday Morning: Cloud cover will \nremain fairly widespread through the evening. But after 3-5 AM, \nradiational cooling beneath light winds and thinning cloud cover \nwill favor nocturnal ground fog development into Monday morning \nacross central and north-central Wisconsin. Surface dewpoints \ngradually rising into the upper 40s to near 50 degrees this \nafternoon will promote boundary layer moisture saturation between \n3-10 AM Monday. NBM statistics and API hourly guidance indicate a \n40 to 60 percent probability of localized dense fog reducing \nvisibilities below 1 mile near Rhinelander, Wausau, and Merrill. \nHigh confidence exists in rapid fog dissipation by 15Z Monday as \nmorning solar heating drives boundary layer mixing.\n\nProlonged Dry And Mild Early Week Pattern: Deep-layer atmospheric\nsubsidence associated with the persistent surface high pressure \nridge will lock in dry and tranquil weather across the forecast \narea through Tuesday afternoon. Inland high temperatures will warm\ninto the mid-70s on Monday, while light onshore lake breezes keep\nLake Michigan shoreline communities slightly cooler in the upper \n60s. NBM ensemble guidance exhibits high confidence with tight \n10th to 90th percentile temperature spreads of only 2 to 3 degrees\nF for daily highs through Monday. \n\nMidweek Frontal System And Widespread Rainfall: Deterministic and\nensemble guidance consensus tracks a slow-moving northern stream \ntrough and advancing surface cold front into the Upper Mississippi\nValley Tuesday night into Wednesday. Strong low-level moisture \ntransport will elevate rain probabilities to 60 to 80 percent \nTuesday night through Wednesday afternoon. NBM probabilistic \nguidance indicates a 50 to 80 percent probability of 48-hour QPF \nexceeding 0.50 inches across the region, with NBM 50th percentile\nliquid accumulation ranging between 0.50 and 1.25 inches. \nSurface- based CAPE values under 300 J/kg and poor shear- moisture\noverlap will keep thunderstorm activity isolated and mitigate \nsevere weather potential.\n\nLate Week Cooling Trend And Frost Potential: Post-frontal cold air\nadvection will usher in a cooler and drier airmass Thursday\nthrough Saturday, dropping daytime highs into the low to mid-60s\narea-wide. As high pressure builds southward from Canada, light\nwinds and clearing skies will promote efficient radiational\ncooling on Friday night. NBM temperature percentiles show\novernight minimum temperatures dropping into the mid to upper 30s\nacross Vilas, Oneida, and Forest counties Friday night. This setup\nyields a 15 to 30 percent probability of frost in sheltered low- \nlying spots across the Northwoods before high pressure begins \nshifting east over the weekend.",
